(350721, 26829, 1880, '<td valign="top">Julia,<br/> <br/>I didn''t get to vote on this image but I feel it is an outstanding image.  The compositional subjects have great placement, the light is very good, and this photograph has emotional impact.  I think good photographs of people, like this one, are much more interesting than photogenic bugs any day. I would have scored this a 10. Congratulations on a wonderful photograph.<br/> <br/>Bob<br/></td>', 'Julia, I didn''t get to vote on this image but I feel it is an outstanding image.  The compositional subjects have great placement, the light is very good, and this photograph has emotional impact.  I think good photographs of people, like this one, are much more interesting than photogenic bugs any day. I would have scored this a 10. Congratulations on a wonderful photograph. Bob', '2003-07-09 02:26:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(351741, 26817, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>Wow this actually fits two very recent challenges both the B Challenge and the Speed Challenge.  <br/> <br/>It is such a cool photo it really does show so much it has a great deal of detail in it.  Your focus is fabulous.  Your bees moving around blurred and then the honeycomb being in such wonderful focus.   <br/> <br/>A very good finish my question is did you try cropping it differently so you would cut out the left top corner?  Although I know it is bees moving it seems to distract from the rest of the photo.<br/> <br/>Nicely done and congrats on the 3rd place<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique Club*  Wow this actually fits two very recent challenges both the B Challenge and the Speed Challenge.   It is such a cool photo it really does show so much it has a great deal of detail in it.  Your focus is fabulous.  Your bees moving around blurred and then the honeycomb being in such wonderful focus.    A very good finish my question is did you try cropping it differently so you would cut out the left top corner?  Although I know it is bees moving it seems to distract from the rest of the photo. Nicely done and congrats on the 3rd place Anna', '2003-07-09 19:42:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(351927, 26947, 2586, '<td valign="top">Didn''t vote on this challenge but just read all the comments about people liking or not liking the softness. I find this portrait very beautiful. Just love the light and composition is fine. It works well soft and it probably would work well if in slightly sharper focus. Now that''s postvote you might consider editing out that white shape that hangs ominously above his head. Doesn''t need to be black, a med to dark grey would work fine, too. You probably already considered that.</td>', 'Didn''t vote on this challenge but just read all the comments about people liking or not liking the softness. I find this portrait very beautiful. Just love the light and composition is fine. It works well soft and it probably would work well if in slightly sharper focus. Now that''s postvote you might consider editing out that white shape that hangs ominously above his head. Doesn''t need to be black, a med to dark grey would work fine, too. You probably already considered that.', '2003-07-09 23:05:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(352126, 26428, 4871, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the votes everyone :) For those that are interested in how this was done, it was a shot that was taken with a 15 second exposure taken in three stages using my Nokia 8250. First stage was with me standing outside the shot totally just putting my hand in to write the B with the phone facing the camera. To get to the second stage I covered the phone with my hand and positioned it, then write the ''lue''. Then for the final stage, the pic of me holding the ''light'', I covered the phone again and had a marker on the ground where I rested my back on the wall, positioned the phone roughly where I thought the ''B'' would start and faced the phone AWAY from the camera, illuminating the wall as well. It took about 120 shots!!!</td>', 'Thanks for the votes everyone :) For those that are interested in how this was done, it was a shot that was taken with a 15 second exposure taken in three stages using my Nokia 8250. First stage was with me standing outside the shot totally just putting my hand in to write the B with the phone facing the camera. To get to the second stage I covered the phone with my hand and positioned it, then write the ''lue''. Then for the final stage, the pic of me holding the ''light'', I covered the phone again and had a marker on the ground where I rested my back on the wall, positioned the phone roughly where I thought the ''B'' would start and faced the phone AWAY from the camera, illuminating the wall as well. It took about 120 shots!!!', '2003-07-10 02:22:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(352141, 26752, 2586, '<td valign="top">I had actually submitted Low Tide for this challenge but at the eleventh hour changed it for a seagull picture. After voting began and i saw the first comment, i saw this image and was sort of shocked. Why in the heck did i submit this? I just went through my image folder for that day and it turns out i submitted the wrong shot! The one i meant to submit was rather similar but much better clarity of the gull and the foreground rocks. Oh well :)</td>', 'I had actually submitted Low Tide for this challenge but at the eleventh hour changed it for a seagull picture. After voting began and i saw the first comment, i saw this image and was sort of shocked. Why in the heck did i submit this? I just went through my image folder for that day and it turns out i submitted the wrong shot! The one i meant to submit was rather similar but much better clarity of the gull and the foreground rocks. Oh well :)', '2003-07-10 03:04:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(352310, 26854, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - First thing I noticed when I saw this picture was that the bug is a little bit small for this shot.  I wanted to zoom in on him but obviously that is not possible.  His placement is too far up in the frame.  This photo I think is applicable to the rule of thirds, put him on the upper left side but more towards the center of the picture.  The banana is also a little dark in color from what I can remember about bananas.  I don''t know if it''d been sitting out awhile or a color adjustment was made post shot.  I like the fact that we can see some of the peel, but I think it might''ve been conducive to show some of the outside of the banana, to add a bit more color into the mix.  Maybe some of the color problem could''ve been alleviated with a little more light.<br/> <br/>Technical - Regarding the lighting, I don''t like the shadow cast on the banana by the peel, but not sure if you could effectively remove it.  Its pretty crisp and clear, no real problems there.  A little more exposure probably sounds easier said than done, considering this insect is alive and probably has the attention span of my 6 year old.  Not a badly taken photo.<br/> <br/>Overall - I don''t think these subjects have the potential to generate a lot of score.  This image is lacking a little in visual appeal, excuse the pun.  There isn''t a lot of color and really nothing extraordinary happening.  I think you did pretty well with this picture in terms of taking it, but there are some things that I would''ve changed had I taken the photo.  But it''s not my photo, so I''m just here to tell you what I think.  Best of Luck in future challenges - Bob</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - First thing I noticed when I saw this picture was that the bug is a little bit small for this shot.  I wanted to zoom in on him but obviously that is not possible.  His placement is too far up in the frame.  This photo I think is applicable to the rule of thirds, put him on the upper left side but more towards the center of the picture.  The banana is also a little dark in color from what I can remember about bananas.  I don''t know if it''d been sitting out awhile or a color adjustment was made post shot.  I like the fact that we can see some of the peel, but I think it might''ve been conducive to show some of the outside of the banana, to add a bit more color into the mix.  Maybe some of the color problem could''ve been alleviated with a little more light. Technical - Regarding the lighting, I don''t like the shadow cast on the banana by the peel, but not sure if you could effectively remove it.  Its pretty crisp and clear, no real problems there.  A little more exposure probably sounds easier said than done, considering this insect is alive and probably has the attention span of my 6 year old.  Not a badly taken photo. Overall - I don''t think these subjects have the potential to generate a lot of score.  This image is lacking a little in visual appeal, excuse the pun.  There isn''t a lot of color and really nothing extraordinary happening.  I think you did pretty well with this picture in terms of taking it, but there are some things that I would''ve changed had I taken the photo.  But it''s not my photo, so I''m just here to tell you what I think.  Best of Luck in future challenges - Bob', '2003-07-10 09:17:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(352739, 26861, 1647, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Composition: I like the composition of your shot.  The two cannons are nicely framed and they make me wonder what is out to the left of the frame.  They do, however, blend heavily into the background and are relatively hard to see.  I like the aspect ratio of this shot and it looks like the shot was taken from low down, which I also like.<br/> <br/>Technical Quality: The black and white conversion let''s your shot down.  It looks like you might have had exposure problems with a very bright sky as well.  It may have been possible to convert different colours to grey differently to provide more contrast between the cannons and the trees.<br/> <br/>Meeting Challenge: I like your approach at meeting the challenge.  It''s not the most literal interpretation, which is actually nice.<br/> <br/>Overall: I really like the composition and content of this shot.  Personally I would try to convert to grey scale again - I''m sure there are some people who could help out with creative ways of doing the conversion (or search the web).  Well done.</td>', 'Critique Club: Composition: I like the composition of your shot.  The two cannons are nicely framed and they make me wonder what is out to the left of the frame.  They do, however, blend heavily into the background and are relatively hard to see.  I like the aspect ratio of this shot and it looks like the shot was taken from low down, which I also like. Technical Quality: The black and white conversion let''s your shot down.  It looks like you might have had exposure problems with a very bright sky as well.  It may have been possible to convert different colours to grey differently to provide more contrast between the cannons and the trees. Meeting Challenge: I like your approach at meeting the challenge.  It''s not the most literal interpretation, which is actually nice. Overall: I really like the composition and content of this shot.  Personally I would try to convert to grey scale again - I''m sure there are some people who could help out with creative ways of doing the conversion (or search the web).  Well done.', '2003-07-10 21:21:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(352801, 26778, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>As I mentioned during the challenge, I really like the composition of this shot.  It very strong catches the eyes and then leads them through the frame.  I guess, technically, "leading lines" are supposed to lead "somewhere," but I like it when they just lead me out of the frame -- kinda like a joke on the viewer almost.  I also like the way the shadows provides a dark "line" through the frame breaking of the blue tones.<br/> <br/>Technically, the focus, lighting, etc is good.  Don''t know that I can comment on that a lot.  I guess what may have been a "downer" for some people is that, while it is technically a good shot, and compositionally interesting (at least for me), it lacks that certain element that gives it a "personality" to the general population.  Though this structure may mean a lot to you, it just doesn''t convey that emotion to the average person and pull them in.  Perhaps if you had put someone or something in  the foreground (a sad woman, a man sitting thoughtfully) in the foreground so that it had a more emotional subject, more people would have been intrigued by this shot.<br/> <br/>If you have questions, or comments, please feel free to PM me.<br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/> <br/>karmat<br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat As I mentioned during the challenge, I really like the composition of this shot.  It very strong catches the eyes and then leads them through the frame.  I guess, technically, "leading lines" are supposed to lead "somewhere," but I like it when they just lead me out of the frame -- kinda like a joke on the viewer almost.  I also like the way the shadows provides a dark "line" through the frame breaking of the blue tones. Technically, the focus, lighting, etc is good.  Don''t know that I can comment on that a lot.  I guess what may have been a "downer" for some people is that, while it is technically a good shot, and compositionally interesting (at least for me), it lacks that certain element that gives it a "personality" to the general population.  Though this structure may mean a lot to you, it just doesn''t convey that emotion to the average person and pull them in.  Perhaps if you had put someone or something in  the foreground (a sad woman, a man sitting thoughtfully) in the foreground so that it had a more emotional subject, more people would have been intrigued by this shot. If you have questions, or comments, please feel free to PM me.Best to you in future challenges. karmat  ', '2003-07-10 23:56:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(353116, 26376, 4564, '<td valign="top">This is an obsolute lovely shot!  I love the composition and the model''s expression! Lovely! Gorgous!</td>', 'This is an obsolute lovely shot!  I love the composition and the model''s expression! Lovely! Gorgous!', '2003-07-11 04:32:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(353618, 26817, 6696, '<td valign="top">Anna, thanks for the critique (my first in a while!).  I did consider cropping out the upper left corner.  I tried and tried - rotated, etc. but I was losing a lot of sharpness and detail that I didn''t think I could compensate for in NeatImage.  <a href="//dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=28319" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Here</a> is one of my other attempts.<br/> <br/>I think I''ll give it another try during my next visit to the Zoo.<br/>Thanks again for the helpful critique!<br/>Jerry</td>', 'Anna, thanks for the critique (my first in a while!).  I did consider cropping out the upper left corner.  I tried and tried - rotated, etc. but I was losing a lot of sharpness and detail that I didn''t think I could compensate for in NeatImage.  Here is one of my other attempts. I think I''ll give it another try during my next visit to the Zoo.Thanks again for the helpful critique!Jerry', '2003-07-11 12:21:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(353708, 26289, 4482, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC!<br/> <br/>WOW! 2 weeks in a row that I get to critique some of my fav shots!!! :o)<br/> <br/>LIGHTING: A beautiful high key shot without having the "blown out" effect that hurts the eyes. Not always easy to get. <br/> <br/>FOCUS: Spot on....<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: The white on white is very pure and clean and works well here. The curves in the bowls makes all the difference in the pic. Your vertical cropping is good but the negative space above the bowls is distracting. I don''t seems to find its purpose there. Maybe to show length?? As for the line in your BG.... didn''t notice it so you did a good job. :o)<br/> <br/>You''re lucky to have 10 identical bowls!!! My dishes are all mismatched!! :oP<br/> <br/>Great job once again!! Keep on inspiring!<br/> <br/>Yanik</td>', 'Greetings from the CC! WOW! 2 weeks in a row that I get to critique some of my fav shots!!! :o) LIGHTING: A beautiful high key shot without having the "blown out" effect that hurts the eyes. Not always easy to get.  FOCUS: Spot on.... COMPOSITION: The white on white is very pure and clean and works well here. The curves in the bowls makes all the difference in the pic. Your vertical cropping is good but the negative space above the bowls is distracting. I don''t seems to find its purpose there. Maybe to show length?? As for the line in your BG.... didn''t notice it so you did a good job. :o) You''re lucky to have 10 identical bowls!!! My dishes are all mismatched!! :oP Great job once again!! Keep on inspiring! Yanik', '2003-07-11 13:47:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(353820, 26944, 6025, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>A very difficult photograph to take correctly. I think the idea is great, but the main problem with this is that the binoculars themselves are too dark. All the detail of this pair of B''s (esp. because they are obviously used a lot) has been lost, which I think is why you didn''t do better than you did (although 5.5 isn''t a bad score at all)<br/> <br/>The only decent advice I can give you is to make sure that your image is lit properly, and try to do your best to bring out all the characteristics of your subject.<br/> <br/>A good idea that faltered slightly on the technical side. <br/> <br/>Keep it up!</td>', 'Critique Club A very difficult photograph to take correctly. I think the idea is great, but the main problem with this is that the binoculars themselves are too dark. All the detail of this pair of B''s (esp. because they are obviously used a lot) has been lost, which I think is why you didn''t do better than you did (although 5.5 isn''t a bad score at all) The only decent advice I can give you is to make sure that your image is lit properly, and try to do your best to bring out all the characteristics of your subject. A good idea that faltered slightly on the technical side.  Keep it up!', '2003-07-11 15:57:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(353825, 26443, 6025, '<td valign="top">A pretty cool photograph you have there. To the point that I find it very difficult to tell you what can be improved with it. The choice of using a polarizer was obviously a good one. The boxcar itself is captured in a cool way. The shadows on the side of the car only help to break up the monotony, which is all good.<br/> <br/>I think that you met the challenge in a good way, and there is not much to be improved in the picture. I guess the only thing that stopped you from winning was that the subject is a little boring - Personally, I thought: "a boxcar. It starts with a B. So what" - there doesn''t actually seem to be any motivation or message behind the image (apart from beginning with a B), which pulls the overall impression down.<br/> <br/>Keep it up though - technically you are sound, and it is only a matter of time before you get another ribbon!</td>', 'A pretty cool photograph you have there. To the point that I find it very difficult to tell you what can be improved with it. The choice of using a polarizer was obviously a good one. The boxcar itself is captured in a cool way. The shadows on the side of the car only help to break up the monotony, which is all good. I think that you met the challenge in a good way, and there is not much to be improved in the picture. I guess the only thing that stopped you from winning was that the subject is a little boring - Personally, I thought: "a boxcar. It starts with a B. So what" - there doesn''t actually seem to be any motivation or message behind the image (apart from beginning with a B), which pulls the overall impression down. Keep it up though - technically you are sound, and it is only a matter of time before you get another ribbon!', '2003-07-11 16:03:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(354005, 26873, 6025, '<td valign="top">Heya! This is your Critique Club message thingie.<br/> <br/>I am partially happy that I got to critique this one, and partially sad. Y''see, I already said good things about it. Simultaneously, I dunno what to tell you about it either.<br/> <br/>It is a truly great photograph. I really do believe that it should have won the challenge, but alas. <br/> <br/>Fabulous idea, superb lightning, and (for once) a border that really stands out, and brings out the best of the photograph<br/> <br/>The only thing I would comment on is that it seems a little oversharpened along the top edge of the glass. A pure nitpick, though, as it does not really distract from the image as a whole.<br/> <br/>I used to have a Pro90 (can''t remember being able to use ISO 50 - odd), and remember the camera to be really good - but I never got images this good out of it :) <br/> <br/>Great effort, my friend - I am sure the ribbons are not far out of reach.<br/> <br/>(but why did you use coors when you call yourself miller? hehe)<br/> <br/>Rock on!<br/> <br/><img alt="'' . substr(''//www.photocritic.org/hidden/dpcman.gif'', strrpos(''//www.photocritic.org/hidden/dpcman.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.photocritic.org/hidden/dpcman.gif"/> - haje</td>', 'Heya! This is your Critique Club message thingie. I am partially happy that I got to critique this one, and partially sad. Y''see, I already said good things about it. Simultaneously, I dunno what to tell you about it either. It is a truly great photograph. I really do believe that it should have won the challenge, but alas.  Fabulous idea, superb lightning, and (for once) a border that really stands out, and brings out the best of the photograph The only thing I would comment on is that it seems a little oversharpened along the top edge of the glass. A pure nitpick, though, as it does not really distract from the image as a whole. I used to have a Pro90 (can''t remember being able to use ISO 50 - odd), and remember the camera to be really good - but I never got images this good out of it :)  Great effort, my friend - I am sure the ribbons are not far out of reach. (but why did you use coors when you call yourself miller? hehe) Rock on!  - haje', '2003-07-11 17:44:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(354029, 26818, 9462, '<td valign="top">If it weren''t for dsidwell i would''ve never known that was a moth! I''ve never seen such a colorful moth.<br/>I like the border... but there''s a spot just above the right wing that bothers me.<br/>Aloha</td>', 'If it weren''t for dsidwell i would''ve never known that was a moth! I''ve never seen such a colorful moth.I like the border... but there''s a spot just above the right wing that bothers me.Aloha', '2003-07-11 18:14:01.000000', NULL, 0),
